From: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 22 Aug 2026 21:00:00 -0500
Subject: [PATCH] DwUsbHostDxe: pump polled bulk IN endpoints from the periodic
 handler

The USB network class drivers receive by polling: every UNDI poll turns
into a synchronous bulk IN with a 1-3 ms timeout
(USB_ETHERNET_BULK_TIMEOUT). On this controller a synchronous transfer
is a busy-wait at TPL_NOTIFY, and an empty bulk IN does not even fail
fast: in buffer DMA mode the core retries NAKed bulk transactions on its
own, the channel never halts, and Wait4Bit spends the caller's entire
timeout before DwHcTransfer gives up through the XFER_NOT_HALTED path.
Polled from MnpSystemPoll every 10 ms that is a steady 10% of the CPU
held above everything at TPL_CALLBACK and below; polled from a TCP
receive loop during the Redfish exchange it approaches all of it. The
same class driver on the RP1 xHCI ports is quick because that hardware
runs transfer rings autonomously by DMA - the CPU never waits on the
wire.

Point the same DWC2 autonomy the right way instead of fighting it. A
dedicated, otherwise unused host channel (4; the driver's fixed
assignments stop at 3, the core has 16) is left armed with a standing IN
request into a driver-owned common buffer. The core performs the NAK
retries by itself, for free. The 1 ms periodic handler checks HCINT for
a halt - one register read per tick while the endpoint is quiet - and on
XFERCOMP parks the completed transfer. A polled receive is then served
from memory, or told "nothing yet" immediately, without ever touching
the wire at TPL_NOTIFY. Transfer boundaries are preserved: a completion
is one short-packet-terminated transfer, exactly what the synchronous
path would have returned, so CDC NTB/frame framing is untouched. The
hardware owns the endpoint's data toggle while the pump runs, and every
delivery mirrors HCTSIZ.PID back into the caller's DataToggle, so a
handover to the classic path stays coherent.

Engagement is by transfer shape, not device knowledge (the host
controller has none): a high-speed bulk IN with no transaction
translator and a caller timeout of at most 8 ms. The short timeout is
the polling-receive signature - the CDC drivers use 1-3 ms, and the
nearest other bulk user, mass storage BOT, uses 3-5 seconds, three
orders of magnitude away. Everything else takes the classic path
unchanged. Persistent error halts (device detached, endpoint stalled)
stand the pump down for 64 polls of the classic path, which surfaces
the real fault to the class driver and quiesces the channel when the
device is simply gone; the pump re-engages by itself if polling
continues against a live endpoint. DwHcQuiesce halts the pump channel
before resetting the core, so nothing is left mastering the bus at
ExitBootServices, and teardown returns the buffer.

Upstream-Status: Pending
Signed-off-by: appkins <nbatkins@gmail.com>
---
--- a/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.c
+++ b/Platform/RaspberryPi/Drivers/DwUsbHostDxe/DwUsbHostDxe.c
@@ -56,6 +56,23 @@
  * https://www.quicklogic.com/assets/pdf/data-sheets/QL-Hi-Speed-USB-2.0-OTG-Controller-Data-Sheet.pdf
  */
 
+/*
+ * Bulk IN pump tunables; the pump itself is described at DwHcPumpBulkIn.
+ *
+ * Channel 4 sits above the driver's fixed channel assignments (DwcHw.h
+ * uses 0-3) on a core with 16. A bulk IN whose caller timeout is at most
+ * DWC2_PUMP_ENGAGE_TIMEOUT_MS is taken to be a polling receive - the CDC
+ * network drivers use 1-3 ms, while the nearest other bulk user (mass
+ * storage BOT) uses 3-5 seconds. After DWC2_PUMP_MAX_ERRORS consecutive
+ * error halts the pump stands down for DWC2_PUMP_COOLDOWN_POLLS polls of
+ * the classic path, which surfaces the fault to the class driver and
+ * quiesces the channel when the device is simply gone.
+ */
+#define DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP            4
+#define DWC2_PUMP_ENGAGE_TIMEOUT_MS     8
+#define DWC2_PUMP_MAX_ERRORS            8
+#define DWC2_PUMP_COOLDOWN_POLLS        64
+
 typedef enum {
   XFER_NOT_HALTED,
   XFER_ERROR,
@@ -1069,6 +1086,322 @@
   return Status;
 }
 
+/*
+ * Bulk IN pump.
+ *
+ * The USB network class drivers receive by polling: NetworkCommon's UNDI
+ * calls the class driver's receive on every poll, and that becomes a
+ * synchronous bulk IN with a millisecond timeout (USB_ETHERNET_BULK_TIMEOUT).
+ * On this controller a synchronous transfer is a busy-wait at TPL_NOTIFY,
+ * and a bulk IN the device has nothing for does not even fail fast: in
+ * buffer DMA mode the core retries NAKed bulk transactions on its own, the
+ * channel never halts, and Wait4Bit burns the caller's entire timeout
+ * before the transfer is abandoned. Polled from MnpSystemPoll every 10 ms
+ * that is a steady tax; polled from a TCP receive loop it is nearly all of
+ * the CPU, and everything below TPL_NOTIFY - BDS included - crawls.
+ *
+ * The same hardware autonomy, pointed the right way, is the fix. Leave one
+ * channel armed with a standing IN request, let the core do the NAK
+ * retries for free, and notice the completed transfer from the 1 ms
+ * periodic handler - one register read per tick. The polled receive is
+ * then served from memory, or told "nothing yet" immediately, without
+ * touching the wire. Transfer boundaries are preserved: a completion is
+ * one short-packet-terminated transfer, exactly what the synchronous path
+ * would have returned, so NTB/frame framing is untouched.
+ *
+ * Only a short-timeout high-speed bulk IN with no transaction translator
+ * engages the pump (see DWC2_PUMP_ENGAGE_TIMEOUT_MS above). Everything
+ * else takes the classic synchronous path unchanged.
+ */
+
+STATIC
+VOID
+DwHcPumpArm (
+  IN  DWUSB_OTGHC_DEV *DwHc
+  )
+{
+  DWUSB_PUMP    *Pump = &DwHc->Pump;
+  SPLIT_CONTROL NoSplit;
+  UINT32        NumPackets;
+
+  ZeroMem (&NoSplit, sizeof (NoSplit));
+  NumPackets = Pump->ArmedLength / (UINT32)Pump->MaximumPacketLength;
+
+  MmioWrite32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCDMA (DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP),
+    (UINTN)Pump->BufferBusAddress);
+
+  DwOtgHcInit (DwHc, DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP, NULL, Pump->DeviceSpeed,
+    Pump->DeviceAddress, Pump->EpAddress, 1, DWC2_HCCHAR_EPTYPE_BULK,
+    (UINT16)Pump->MaximumPacketLength, &NoSplit);
+
+  MmioWrite32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCTSIZ (DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP),
+    (Pump->ArmedLength << DWC2_HCTSIZ_XFERSIZE_OFFSET) |
+    (NumPackets << DWC2_HCTSIZ_PKTCNT_OFFSET) |
+    (Pump->Pid << DWC2_HCTSIZ_PID_OFFSET));
+
+  MmioAndThenOr32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCCHAR (DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP),
+    ~(DWC2_HCCHAR_MULTICNT_MASK |
+      DWC2_HCCHAR_CHEN |
+      DWC2_HCCHAR_CHDIS),
+      ((1 << DWC2_HCCHAR_MULTICNT_OFFSET) |
+        DWC2_HCCHAR_CHEN));
+
+  Pump->State = DwUsbPumpArmed;
+}
+
+STATIC
+VOID
+DwHcPumpHalt (
+  IN  DWUSB_OTGHC_DEV *DwHc
+  )
+{
+  DWUSB_PUMP *Pump = &DwHc->Pump;
+  EFI_TPL    Tpl;
+  UINTN      Waited;
+
+  Tpl = gBS->RaiseTPL (TPL_NOTIFY);
+
+  if (Pump->State == DwUsbPumpArmed) {
+    if ((MmioRead32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCINT (DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP)) &
+         DWC2_HCINT_CHHLTD) == 0) {
+      MmioOr32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCCHAR (DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP),
+        DWC2_HCCHAR_CHDIS);
+      for (Waited = 0; Waited < 2000; Waited += 10) {
+        if ((MmioRead32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCINT (DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP)) &
+             DWC2_HCINT_CHHLTD) != 0) {
+          break;
+        }
+
+        MicroSecondDelay (10);
+      }
+    }
+
+    MmioWrite32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCINT (DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP), 0x3FFF);
+  }
+
+  Pump->State = DwUsbPumpIdle;
+  Pump->ReadyLength = 0;
+
+  gBS->RestoreTPL (Tpl);
+}
+
+/*
+ * Classify a halted pump channel. Runs from the TPL_NOTIFY periodic
+ * handler every millisecond and from DwHcPumpBulkIn; while the channel is
+ * armed and the endpoint is NAKing, the cost is the single HCINT read.
+ */
+STATIC
+VOID
+DwHcPumpHarvest (
+  IN  DWUSB_OTGHC_DEV *DwHc
+  )
+{
+  DWUSB_PUMP *Pump = &DwHc->Pump;
+  UINT32     Hcint;
+  UINT32     Hctsiz;
+  UINT32     Sub;
+
+  if (Pump->State != DwUsbPumpArmed) {
+    return;
+  }
+
+  Hcint = MmioRead32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCINT (DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP));
+  if ((Hcint & DWC2_HCINT_CHHLTD) == 0) {
+    return;
+  }
+
+  if ((Hcint & DWC2_HCINT_XFERCOMP) != 0) {
+    Hctsiz = MmioRead32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCTSIZ (DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP));
+    Sub = (Hctsiz & DWC2_HCTSIZ_XFERSIZE_MASK) >> DWC2_HCTSIZ_XFERSIZE_OFFSET;
+    Pump->Pid = (Hctsiz & DWC2_HCTSIZ_PID_MASK) >> DWC2_HCTSIZ_PID_OFFSET;
+    Pump->Errors = 0;
+
+    if (Sub >= Pump->ArmedLength) {
+      /*
+       * A completion that moved no data (a bare ZLP) carries no frame
+       * block; rearm rather than waking the class driver for nothing.
+       */
+      DwHcPumpArm (DwHc);
+      return;
+    }
+
+    ArmDataSynchronizationBarrier ();
+    Pump->ReadyLength = Pump->ArmedLength - Sub;
+    Pump->State = DwUsbPumpReady;
+    return;
+  }
+
+  if ((Hcint & (DWC2_HCINT_NAK | DWC2_HCINT_FRMOVRUN)) != 0) {
+    /*
+     * Halted without data. Not an error: rearm, the next tick looks
+     * again.
+     */
+    DwHcPumpArm (DwHc);
+    return;
+  }
+
+  /*
+   * XACTERR, BBLERR, STALL, AHBERR... Retry a few times - transaction
+   * errors are recoverable - then stand down and let the classic
+   * synchronous path surface the condition to the class driver, which is
+   * also what quiesces the pump once the device is detached and the class
+   * driver stops polling.
+   */
+  Pump->Errors++;
+  if (Pump->Errors < DWC2_PUMP_MAX_ERRORS) {
+    DwHcPumpArm (DwHc);
+    return;
+  }
+
+  DEBUG ((DEBUG_INFO,
+    "%a: dev %u ep 0x%x HCINT 0x%x after %u error halts; standing down\n",
+    __func__, Pump->DeviceAddress, Pump->EpAddress, Hcint, Pump->Errors));
+  MmioWrite32 (DwHc->DwUsbBase + HCINT (DWC2_HC_CHANNEL_PUMP), 0x3FFF);
+  Pump->State = DwUsbPumpIdle;
+  Pump->Errors = 0;
+  Pump->Cooldown = DWC2_PUMP_COOLDOWN_POLLS;
+}
+
+STATIC
+EFI_STATUS
+DwHcPumpBulkIn (
+  IN     DWUSB_OTGHC_DEV *DwHc,
+  IN     UINT8           DeviceAddress,
+  IN     UINT8           EndPointAddress,
+  IN     UINT8           DeviceSpeed,
+  IN     UINTN           MaximumPacketLength,
+  IN OUT VOID            *Data,
+  IN OUT UINTN           *DataLength,
+  IN OUT UINT8           *DataToggle,
+  OUT    UINT32          *TransferResult,
+  OUT    BOOLEAN         *Handled
+  )
+{
+  DWUSB_PUMP *Pump = &DwHc->Pump;
+  EFI_STATUS Status;
+  EFI_TPL    Tpl;
+  UINT8      EpNum;
+  UINT32     Request;
+  UINT32     NumPackets;
+  UINTN      Bytes;
+  UINTN      BufferSize;
+
+  *Handled = TRUE;
+  EpNum = EndPointAddress & 0x0F;
+
+  Tpl = gBS->RaiseTPL (TPL_NOTIFY);
+
+  /*
+   * A different endpoint than the pump is serving means the device was
+   * re-enumerated or another poller appeared; the newcomer wins.
+   */
+  if ((Pump->State != DwUsbPumpIdle) &&
+      ((Pump->DeviceAddress != DeviceAddress) ||
+       (Pump->EpAddress != EpNum) ||
+       (Pump->MaximumPacketLength != MaximumPacketLength))) {
+    DwHcPumpHalt (DwHc);
+    Pump->Errors = 0;
+    Pump->Cooldown = 0;
+  }
+
+  if (Pump->State == DwUsbPumpIdle) {
+    if (Pump->Cooldown != 0) {
+      Pump->Cooldown--;
+      *Handled = FALSE;
+      gBS->RestoreTPL (Tpl);
+      return EFI_DEVICE_ERROR;
+    }
+
+    if (Pump->Buffer == NULL) {
+      Status = DmaAllocateBuffer (EfiBootServicesData,
+                 EFI_SIZE_TO_PAGES (DWC2_DATA_BUF_SIZE),
+                 (VOID **)&Pump->Buffer);
+      if (EFI_ERROR (Status)) {
+        Pump->Buffer = NULL;
+        *Handled = FALSE;
+        gBS->RestoreTPL (Tpl);
+        return Status;
+      }
+
+      BufferSize = EFI_PAGES_TO_SIZE (EFI_SIZE_TO_PAGES (DWC2_DATA_BUF_SIZE));
+      Status = DmaMap (MapOperationBusMasterCommonBuffer, Pump->Buffer,
+                 &BufferSize, &Pump->BufferBusAddress, &Pump->BufferMapping);
+      if (EFI_ERROR (Status)) {
+        DmaFreeBuffer (EFI_SIZE_TO_PAGES (DWC2_DATA_BUF_SIZE), Pump->Buffer);
+        Pump->Buffer = NULL;
+        *Handled = FALSE;
+        gBS->RestoreTPL (Tpl);
+        return Status;
+      }
+    }
+
+    Request = (UINT32)MIN (*DataLength,
+                (UINTN)(DWC2_MAX_TRANSFER_SIZE - MaximumPacketLength + 1));
+    NumPackets = (Request + (UINT32)MaximumPacketLength - 1) /
+                 (UINT32)MaximumPacketLength;
+    if (NumPackets == 0) {
+      *Handled = FALSE;
+      gBS->RestoreTPL (Tpl);
+      return EFI_INVALID_PARAMETER;
+    }
+
+    if (NumPackets > DWC2_MAX_PACKET_COUNT) {
+      NumPackets = DWC2_MAX_PACKET_COUNT;
+    }
+
+    Pump->DeviceAddress = DeviceAddress;
+    Pump->EpAddress = EpNum;
+    Pump->DeviceSpeed = DeviceSpeed;
+    Pump->MaximumPacketLength = MaximumPacketLength;
+    Pump->ArmedLength = NumPackets * (UINT32)MaximumPacketLength;
+    Pump->Pid = (UINT32)(*DataToggle << 1);
+    Pump->Errors = 0;
+
+    DEBUG ((DEBUG_INFO, "%a: pumping dev %u ep 0x%02x mps %u request %u\n",
+      __func__, DeviceAddress, EndPointAddress,
+      (UINT32)MaximumPacketLength, Pump->ArmedLength));
+
+    DwHcPumpArm (DwHc);
+  }
+
+  /*
+   * A completion may have been sitting since the last tick; look now
+   * rather than costing the caller a poll interval of latency.
+   */
+  DwHcPumpHarvest (DwHc);
+
+  if (Pump->State == DwUsbPumpReady) {
+    Bytes = MIN (*DataLength, (UINTN)Pump->ReadyLength);
+    CopyMem (Data, Pump->Buffer, Bytes);
+    *DataLength = Bytes;
+    *DataToggle = (UINT8)(Pump->Pid >> 1);
+    *TransferResult = EFI_USB_NOERROR;
+    DwHcPumpArm (DwHc);
+    gBS->RestoreTPL (Tpl);
+    return EFI_SUCCESS;
+  }
+
+  if (Pump->State == DwUsbPumpArmed) {
+    /*
+     * Nothing yet. The synchronous path would now busy-wait the whole
+     * timeout at TPL_NOTIFY against a NAKing endpoint; the hardware is
+     * already doing those retries, so report the timeout immediately.
+     */
+    *DataLength = 0;
+    *TransferResult = EFI_USB_ERR_TIMEOUT;
+    gBS->RestoreTPL (Tpl);
+    return EFI_TIMEOUT;
+  }
+
+  /*
+   * The harvest just stood the pump down; let the classic path run and
+   * report the fault.
+   */
+  *Handled = FALSE;
+  gBS->RestoreTPL (Tpl);
+  return EFI_DEVICE_ERROR;
+}
+
